---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Param from "../../../components/Param.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="depth-map"
  title="Depth"
  description="When to choose Depth, one of Deptheos's three methods, and what its Smoothing, Radius and Blend Range settings do to the picture."
  hasLabs
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Depth</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Depth first decides, for every spot of the picture, which frame has it sharpest: a map of
      depth. Then it builds the picture from that map, taking each spot from its frame.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose it for plain out-of-focus backgrounds, smooth surfaces, and something bright beside something dark.
      Colors stay true to the source frames, and the out-of-focus glow that the pyramid method can let in stays
      out. Each spot gets one depth, though, so a hair in front of a distant background can come out soft, and
      so can the line where one part stands in front of another. The method treats frame numbers as depths, so
      the frames must be in focus order, and it reads every frame twice, which makes it slower than the
      pyramid. <a href="/docs/methods/">Choosing a method</a> shows the three methods side by side.
    </p>
    <details id="depth">
      <summary>Look through a depth map, frame by frame (66 frames, about 4 MB)</summary>
      <p>
        The depth is a frame number, not a distance: early frames for the parts nearest the lens, late frames
        for the parts farthest away.
      </p>
      <figure class="lab" id="depth-map" data-lab="depthmap" data-assets="depthmap" data-frames-from="sharpest" data-map="default-head-map.png" data-center="600,560">
        <div class="lab-body">
          <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/sharpest/frame-01.jpg" loading="lazy" width="768" height="768" alt="The right side of the hornet's head, with an antenna crossing in front of it, against the green" /></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Frames the app aligned from the hornet sample stack, beside the depth map it made, at 100%. Move
          through the frames, or tap the map to jump to the frame it chose there. The map gives each frame a
          color, from blue for frame 1 to red for the last, and lights the parts that frame has sharpest.
        </figcaption>
        <div class="lab-why">
          <p>
            The antenna crosses in front of the hornet's head, nearer the lens, so the map gives it an earlier
            frame: it lights up in the twenties, and the hairy head behind it in the fifties. Whatever lights up
            is what is sharp in the frame beside it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The plain green lights up at frame 1. Nothing there is sharp in any frame, so its depth is close to
            a guess, but the scores are pooled over a patch (see Radius, below), so the whole background takes
            one guess instead of a different frame at every pixel. Which frame it takes barely changes the
            picture, since there every frame is about equally blurred.
          </p>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </details>
  </div>

  <section id="settings">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where the settings are</h2>
      <p>
        Smoothing is Depth's main slider, with Radius under it. Blend Range sits under <em>Advanced</em>,
        below them. In the pipeline, <strong>Radius</strong> first scores sharpness over a patch to pick each
        spot's depth, <strong>Smoothing</strong> fills in unsure areas, and <strong>Blend Range</strong> decides
        how tightly to snap each spot to whole frames. Start at the defaults and change one setting at a time,
        comparing the same place. Double-click a slider's name to put it back to its default, or click
        <em>Reset to Defaults</em> under the sliders to put back all three.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="smoothing">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Smoothing</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>What it does.</strong> Evens out the depth map where it is unsure. At every spot the method
        measures how far the sharpest frame's score stands above the average of all frames—high where one
        frame is clearly in focus, near zero where every frame looks alike, such as a plain background.
        Smoothing leaves the confident spots alone and lets the unsure ones take their depth from their
        neighbors. It runs from <Param path="ranges.depth.smoothing.min" />, off, to
        <Param path="ranges.depth.smoothing.max" />, default
        <Param path="defaults.depth.smoothing" />; the slider's ends read Narrow outlines and Even
        backgrounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When to change it.</strong> Raise it to even out the map where it is unsure. Lower it, toward
        off, to keep the outline of a subject against a distant background narrow: raised, Smoothing spreads
        that outline into a wider band of in-between depths.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it costs.</strong> The picture changes far less than the map, because the unsure areas are
        the ones where every frame is about equally blurred. It cannot fix a confident wrong pick, such as a
        thin hair outvoted by the background around it; that is Radius's job. (Pyramid's <em>Smoothness</em>
        blends more frames across the whole picture, whereas Depth's <em>Smoothing</em> only fills in uncertain
        spots of the depth map.)
      </p>
      <details class="numbers">
        <summary>In numbers</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The confidence</strong> is the best score minus the mean over the frames, rescaled on each
            stack to run from 0 to 1, with the middle values lifted. It is a rule of thumb, not a probability:
            two frames sharp at different depths, say a hair and the leg behind it, can make a large gap with no
            clear winner.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The smoothing</strong> sweeps along each row in both directions, then each column, and the
            whole runs twice. At a confident spot the running depth moves almost all the way to the spot's own
            depth; at an unsure one only part of the way, so the depth before it carries on.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scoring">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Radius</h2>
      <p>
        The obvious way to stack is to take each pixel from the frame that scores sharpest there. It is noisy.
        The figure shows that pick, made for this page with the score the pyramid method uses for its finest
        detail, and the same pick with the frames compared over squares around each pixel instead.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure
      class="lab"
      id="pick"
      data-lab="stops"
      data-assets="pick"
      data-stops="w1=1 px|w8=8 px|w64=64 px"
      data-stops-label="Compare frames over squares of"
      data-panels="{stop}.jpg=The picture"
      data-center="460,260"
    >
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/pick/w1.jpg" width="768" height="768" alt="The per-pixel pick at the hornet's head: the green background beside the hairs is grainy" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The hornet's head, each pixel copied from the frame that scores best there, with the frames compared
        over squares of three sizes. The app does not stack this way. Look at the green just above the hairs.
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="w1">
        <p>
          Pixel by pixel, the pick is noisy. In the plain green no frame has any detail, every frame scores about
          the same, and each pixel takes a different one, so the green turns grainy.
          The grain is worst just beside the hairs, where their blur lies over the green in some frames and not in
          others.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="w8" hidden>
        <p>
          Over a square, neighboring pixels see nearly the same area, so they tend to pick the same frame. The
          grain grows into blotches, but it does not go.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="w64" hidden>
        <p>
          Big squares remove the grain: whole patches now come from one frame. But a square that holds two
          depths, a hair and the green far behind it, can pick only one frame, so the green around the hairs is
          copied from frames where the hairs lie over it as a blur. And where the pick changes from one frame to
          another there is a hard seam across the green.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        So Depth decides each spot's depth over a patch around it, and Radius is the size of that
        patch: the frames' scores are averaged over it before they are compared. The seams of the last picture
        above do not appear in the app's result, because it blends the frames either side of a change in depth
        level by level, as the pyramid does. The pyramid method has no Radius: its levels of detail set the
        area each choice covers, from a few pixels in the finest level to hundreds in the coarsest.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure
      class="lab"
      id="radius"
      data-lab="stops"
      data-assets="radius"
      data-stops="r1=1|r8=8, the default|r50=50"
      data-stops-label="Radius"
      data-start="r8"
      data-panels="{stop}-hairs.jpg=The result|frames:{stop}-hairs-map.png=The depth map"
      data-layout="column"
      data-center="560,470"
    >
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/radius/r8-hairs.jpg" width="1024" height="768" alt="Hairs on the top of the hornet's head against the green, at the default Radius" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Results the app made from the hornet sample stack, at 100%: hairs on the top of the head against the
        green, stacked at three Radius settings, above their depth maps. Watch the thin hairs as it grows.
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="r1" hidden>
        <p>
          At 1 each spot decides almost on its own. The hairs are crisp, and in the depth map each hair has its
          own depth. The green, where no frame is sharp, is speckled with picks; in the picture it looks the same
          as at the other settings, since there every frame is about equally blurred.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="r8">
        <p>
          At the default the hairs are still crisp, and the green's picks have merged into patches.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="r50" hidden>
        <p>
          At 50 the hairs turn soft and fade into the green. The patch is far wider than a hair, so each hair is
          outvoted by the background around it and takes the background's depth; the picture then takes the
          hair from frames where it is out of focus. The depth map is a few smooth bands.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>What it does.</strong> Sets how large an area decides each spot's depth together, from
        <Param path="ranges.depth.radius.min" /> to <Param path="ranges.depth.radius.max" />,
        default <Param path="defaults.depth.radius" />. A larger patch decides more steadily, but
        anything much thinner than the patch is outvoted by what surrounds it. The slider's ends read Thin
        detail and Steadier.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When to change it.</strong> Thin hairs and bristles come out soft when Radius is large; lower it
        if they do. Below the default the picture changes little: at 1 and 8 the figure's hairs are equally
        crisp, and only the maps differ. Raise it if faint texture, such as a see-through wing, is lost beside a strong edge: with a
        small patch the edge's blur can outscore the texture, and a larger patch lets the texture's many weak
        votes win.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it costs.</strong> Too large, and thin things take the depth of what is behind them, as at
        50 above. Too small, and the map is speckled where nothing is sharp; the figure shows that this barely
        changes the picture there.
      </p>
      <details class="numbers">
        <summary>In numbers</summary>
        <p>
          The map is made on a gray copy at <Param path="constants.depth.map_scale" scale={100} />%
          of the frame's width and height. At the default Radius the patch is a Gaussian with σ =
          <Param path="at.depth.default.post_blur_sigma" digits={1} /> pixels of that copy,
          reaching <Param path="at.depth.default.post_blur_half_width" /> pixels each side, twice that
          in the photo: many times the thickness of one of the hornet's hairs. The patch grows in proportion to
          Radius.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="snapping">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Blend Range</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>What it does.</strong> After smoothing, many depths fall between two frames: a spot at depth 20.4
        would mix frames 20 and 21, 60 to 40, and a mix can be softer than either frame. Blend Range sets how hard
        each depth is pulled toward the nearest whole frame, so that most spots come from one frame and only
        narrow strips mix two. It runs from <Param path="ranges.depth.blend_range.min" /> to
        <Param path="ranges.depth.blend_range.max" /> (default
        <Param path="defaults.depth.blend_range" />); lower values snap harder, and
        <Param path="ranges.depth.blend_range.max" /> turns snapping off. The slider's ends read One frame per
        spot and Mixed, softer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When to change it.</strong> Rarely. It only moves weight between neighboring frames, so its
        effect on the picture is small. Lower it to take more of the picture whole from single frames; raise it
        to mix more spots from the two frames either side of their depth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
